{
  "review": {
    "score": 0.69,
    "summary": "InterCode formalizes interactive coding as agents issuing code actions, receiving execution feedback, and earning task rewards, and provides Bash and SQL environments built from existing datasets. Its evaluations report higher success rates with interaction than in single-turn settings across the tested models, including a large SQL gain for GPT-4. The framework and initial benchmark have clear practical value, though the evidence covers only two substantial environments and the CTF demonstration is tiny. Important gaps remain: 15 pages were uninspected, several inspected pages are partial, and the supplied excerpts do not expose all experiment details or the full limitations discussion.",
    "observations": [
      {
        "kind": "strength",
        "claim": "The paper specifies a concrete interactive task formulation, including code actions and execution feedback, rather than treating coding solely as static generation.",
        "page": 4,
        "quote": "Given a coding instruction u ∈ U in natural language, an agent issues code or a specialsubmit keyword as an action at ∈ A."
      },
      {
        "kind": "strength",
        "claim": "The reported results support an advantage for multi-turn interaction on these benchmark tasks: GPT-4's SQL success rate rises substantially, and the tables report results across task difficulty and Bash file systems.",
        "page": 7,
        "quote": "gpt-4 19.8 7.2 4.6 3.0 9.1 87.5 76.7 66.7 52.4 73.7"
      },
      {
        "kind": "limitation",
        "claim": "The paper acknowledges limited current environment coverage and a very small CTF demonstration. These are scope boundaries for the evaluated release, not proof that the framework cannot be extended.",
        "page": 26,
        "quote": "With that said, this demonstration currently only has four task instances."
      }
    ],
    "unresolved_questions": [
      "How robust are the reported interaction gains to alternative task sampling, reward definitions, and evaluation settings?",
      "How well do the findings transfer beyond the two implemented Bash and SQL environments, given that the CTF example contains only four instances?",
      "What safeguards beyond Docker isolation are required for agents that might transfer from simulated tasks to real systems?"
    ]
  },
  "ledger": [
    {
      "check_id": "C1",
      "status": "supported",
      "finding": "InterCode defines an action/observation/reward formulation, describes Docker-based environments, and demonstrates implementations for Bash and SQL. Extensibility is supported by the modular design and the two implementations, but broader generality is not established by those examples alone.",
      "support": [
        "E11",
        "E10"
      ],
      "counterevidence": [],
      "unknown": "The inspected excerpts do not permit independent assessment of Docker isolation guarantees or the full interface implementation."
    },
    {
      "check_id": "C2",
      "status": "supported",
      "finding": "The paper reports single-turn and multi-turn success rates across seven models, SQL difficulty levels, and four Bash file systems. The tables show higher overall multi-turn success for every listed model in both environments, including GPT-4 SQL rising from 9.1% to 73.7%. These results support the reported benefit on these tasks, but do not by themselves establish generality beyond them.",
      "support": [
        "E20",
        "E21",
        "E22"
      ],
      "counterevidence": [],
      "unknown": "The supplied excerpts do not include all evaluation details, such as the full prompting-strategy comparison table or all robustness analyses."
    },
    {
      "check_id": "C3",
      "status": "mixed",
      "finding": "The paper states that the implemented environments are currently limited to Bash and SQL, notes that the CTF demonstration has four instances, and warns about risks from unrestricted actions and sim-to-real transfer. These are explicit scope and safety caveats, but the limitations discussion was only partially observed.",
      "support": [
        "E3",
        "E7",
        "E8"
      ],
      "counterevidence": [],
      "unknown": "The limitation excerpts are truncated, and most of the discussion section was not inspected."
    }
  ],
  "changes": "Updated C2 based on pages 5–7: the observed tables do provide quantitative support for interaction gains on the tested tasks, so the earlier claim that main results were unavailable no longer applies. Replaced the hyphenation-ambiguous quote with an exact formulation quote. Kept the score moderate because generalization and some evaluation details remain uncertain; the observation about the small CTF set is explicitly framed as a scope limitation."
}
